(Proof problem.) Points $H$, $K$, $M$ are chosen on the sides of a triangle $ABC$ so that $AH$ is an altitude, $BK$ is an angle bisector and $CM$ is a median. Let $D$ be the intersection of $AH$ and $BK$, and $E$ the intersection of $HM$ and $BK$. Knowing that $KD = 2$, $DE = 1$, $EB = 3$:
(i) prove that $HM$ is parallel to $AC$;
(ii) prove that $AB = AC$;
(iii) prove that $AB = BC$.

(Numerical answer.) Determine the number of quadruples of integers (not necessarily distinct) between $1$ and $12$ (endpoints included) satisfying all the following conditions:
- the sum of the first two numbers is even;
- the sum of the first three numbers is a multiple of $3$;
- the sum of the four numbers is a multiple of $4$.
(Two quadruples that differ even only in the order of the summands are to be regarded as distinct.)

(Proof problem.) Let $k \ge 1$ be a natural number. Determine, in terms of $k$, the number of positive integers $n$ with the following properties:
(a) in base ten they are written with $k$ digits, all odd;
(b) they are divisible by $5$, and the quotient $\frac{n}{5}$, written in base ten, again has $k$ digits, all odd.

(Proof problem.) Let $ABCD$ be a quadrilateral; call $E$ the intersection (other than $A$) of the circles with diameters $AB$ and $AC$, and $F$ the intersection (again other than $A$) of the circles with diameters $AC$ and $AD$. Prove that:
(a) if $\angle EAD = 90^\circ$ then $BC$ is parallel to $AD$;
(b) if $\angle EAD = \angle FAB = 90^\circ$ then $ABCD$ is a parallelogram;
(c) if $ABCD$ is a parallelogram then $\angle EAD = \angle FAB = 90^\circ$.

(Numerical answer.) Determine the largest two-digit number such that:
a) it is a prime number;
b) interchanging its two digits it is still a prime number;
c) the product of its two digits is a prime number.

(Numerical answer.) To steal a precious jewel, a thief has to discover the code that opens the door of the safe. The information he managed to obtain is the following:
– the code is a number;
– every subsequence of consecutive digits of the code (that is, each digit taken singly, each pair of digits, etc., up to the whole number) represents a prime number (for example, $217$ is not good, because $1$ is not a prime and $21$ is not a prime);
– the code is the largest number having this property.
What is the secret code to open the safe?

(Proof problem.) Let $ABC$ be an acute triangle; let $O$ be its circumcentre and let $P$, $Q$ be the points (different from $A$) at which, respectively, the altitude from the vertex $A$ and the extension of $AO$ meet the circumcircle of $ABC$.
(a) Prove that the angles $\angle BAP$ and $\angle QAC$ are congruent;
(b) prove that the triangles $BCP$ and $CBQ$ are congruent;
(c) prove that, if $M$ and $N$ are the midpoints of $AB$ and $AC$, the area of the quadrilateral $ABPC$ is four times the area of the quadrilateral $AMON$.

(Proof problem.) Determine all triples of strictly positive integers $(a, b, c)$ such that
- $a \le b \le c$;
- $\gcd(a, b, c) = 1$;
- $a$ divides $b+c$, $b$ divides $c+a$ and $c$ divides $a+b$.

(Proof problem.) Let $ABCD$ be a convex quadrilateral such that $AB = AC = AD$ and $BC < CD$. The bisector of the angle $\angle BAD$ meets $CD$ internally at $M$ and the extension of $BC$ at $N$. Prove that
(a) the quadrilateral $ABCM$ is cyclic;
(b) the triangles $ANB$ and $ABM$ are similar.

(Proof problem.) Let $ABCD$ be a rectangle with $AB > BC$ and let $\omega$ be its circumscribed circle. Let $E$ and $F$ be respectively the intersections (other than $A$) of the bisector of the angle $\angle BAD$ with the side $CD$ and with the circle $\omega$. The perpendicular to $DF$ through $E$ meets the chord $DF$ at $G$ and the arc $DF$ not containing $C$ at the point $H$. Prove that:
(a) the segments $DF$ and $FB$ have the same length;
(b) the triangles $DEG$ and $DHG$ are congruent;
(c) the segments $HF$ and $FC$ are equal.

(Proof problem.) Let $ABC$ be a triangle, let $r$ be the internal bisector of the acute angle $\angle BAC$ and let $K$ be the projection of $B$ onto $r$, $L$ the projection of $K$ onto $AB$ and $D$ the reflection of $B$ in $L$. Call finally $H$ the foot of the altitude of the triangle $ABC$ from $B$. Prove that:
(a) $BH = 2LK$;
(b) $KA$ bisects the angle $\angle HKD$;
(c) the triangle $ADH$ is isosceles.

(Proof problem.) Given a $2n\times2n$ table, a subset $S$ of its cells is called labyrinthine if it has the following properties:
• from each cell of $S$ it is possible to move to every other cell of $S$ moving only from cells of $S$ to adjacent cells of $S$ (horizontally or vertically, that is, having a side in common with the cell of origin);
• it is not possible, starting from a cell of $S$, to return to the same cell moving only to adjacent cells of $S$ and without ever crossing the same side twice.
(a) Prove that if $S$ is labyrinthine then it cannot have more than $3n^2$ cells;
(b) exhibit a labyrinthine set of $12$ cells for $n = 2$;
(c) call perimeter of $S$ the number of sides adjacent to at least one cell of $S$, but not to two cells of $S$. If $S$ is labyrinthine and consists of $k$ cells, what is its perimeter?
(d) Prove that there is no labyrinthine set of $300$ cells if $n = 10$.

(Numerical answer.) Paola's house is at the point $P$ of a long straight road. Paola trains along the road alternating stretches of $5$ minutes of running at $13$ km/h with stretches of $3$ minutes of walking at $5$ km/h. Having finished a stretch of running $100$ m from $P$, to end the training she decides to:
• walk towards $P$ for a stretch $x$ metres long, with $x < 100$;
• change direction and finish the $3$ minutes of walking moving away from $P$;
• run for $5$ minutes continuing to move away from $P$, then change direction and finish the training with $3$ minutes of walking and $5$ of running towards $P$.
What must $x$ be for the training to end exactly in front of Paola's house?
